      "rationale": "The grouping pipeline's `_group_matching` must recurse into already-grouped containers of a *different* type to find nested constructs of the target type - this is explicitly documented by the in-code comment \"Check inside previously grouped (i.e. parenthesis) if group of different type is inside (i.e., case)\". The current code's recursion guard is `isinstance(token, cls)` instead of `not isinstance(token, cls)`, so a CASE expression wrapped in parentheses is never recursed into and is left ungrouped (its keywords stay flat instead of becoming a single `sql.Case` node), which the first test catches directly via structural inspection and via `get_cases()`. The second test pins down the adjacent, correctly-working behaviour (pure nested parentheses within the documented `MAX_GROUPING_DEPTH` cap must still parse quickly and without error), guarding against the fix accidentally reintroducing the quadratic CPU blow-up or the depth cap firing too early, both described in the CHANGELOG's DoS-protection section.",
